{"name":"Supernova Residues","hex":"#d9ece9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"supernova","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bright and powerful explosion of a massive star which is sudden but brief, and causes the ejection of most or all of the star's matter and the emission of radiation; afterwards, the star becomes a black hole or a neutron star, or is destroyed. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something which is brilliant or explosive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/supernova"},{"term":"residues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of residue"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/residues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Supernova","blocks":[{"text":"A supernova is a powerful and luminous explosion of a star. A supernova occurs during the last evolutionary stages of a massive star, or when a white dwarf is triggered into runaway nuclear fusion. The original object, called the progenitor, either collapses to a neutron star or black hole, or is completely destroyed to form a diffuse nebula. The peak optical luminosity of a supernova can be comparable to that of an entire galaxy before fading over several weeks or months."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/SN1994D.jpg/330px-SN1994D.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supernova"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Crab_Nebula.jpg/500px-Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Crab_Nebula.jpg/1280px-Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Crab_Nebula.jpg/500px-Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link"